# 0116 — Vault not found: the extension should say so and offer to initialize one

**Column**: done — closed 2026-11-25
**Opened**: 2026-11-25
**Related**: [the extension's tracker](../../status/vsc-ext.hmd),
[the per-card discovery card](0108-vault-root-discovery-per-card.hmd),
[the rename card](0023-rename-wiki-to-location-path.hmd),
[[hmd-lang-spec#the-namespace]], [[hmd-lang-spec#project-configuration]]

## What

A user reported this against `hypermarkdown.hmd` 0.5.2 on Windows. They opened a
`.hmd` card in a folder with no `.hmd/` directory, and the extension gave no
guidance on making a vault. They had to find the setup in the README and do it
by hand, and it went wrong in three ways:

- They created `.hmd/` after the card was open, and nothing changed until they
  ran **Rebuild Index** or reloaded the window. The extension caches vault
  discovery and does not notice a new marker.
- They ran an HQL cell first, and it failed with
  `hql: <workspace folder>: not a vault: it holds no .hmd/`. The message names
  the problem and not the fix.
- They could not find an init command in the extension.

## The rule is git's

The specification makes a vault work the way a git repository does:

- A **vault** is the directory that contains `.hmd/`. `.hmd/config.toml` is
  optional, so an empty `.hmd/` is a complete vault.
- When no vault is found and no explicit namespace root is set, an
  implementation **MUST report an error and MUST NOT read the documents**.

Git does not work in a folder that was never `git init`-ed, and it says so.
HyperMarkDown does not work in a folder with no `.hmd/` above it, and it should
say so the same way: **vault not found**, followed by how to initialize one.

The Python CLI already does this. `hmd lint` with no `.hmd/` above it fails and
says to run `hmd init` or pass `--root`. `hql` does it too, which is the error
the reporter saw.

## The extension breaks the rule

The extension does not fail. When no `.hmd/` is above a card, `folderRoot` in
`tools/hmd-vsc-ext/src/workspaceHost.ts` adopts the workspace folder as the
vault and reads the cards anyway. The README advertises this as "a bare
directory of cards works with no setup at all". So the preview draws a card
that has no vault, and the first tool that follows the specification, `hql`,
refuses the same card. The two halves of the editor disagree about whether the
card has a vault.

The fix:

- **No vault, no reading.** Drop the fallback. A card with no `.hmd/` above it,
  up to the workspace folder, has no vault. The only exception is the explicit
  namespace root the specification requires an implementation to offer, which
  in the extension is the `hyperMarkdown.root` setting. When the user sets it,
  that root is used.
- **The error offers the fix.** Instead of drawing the card, the extension
  shows an error with a blue primary action:

  > Vault not found for `card.hmd`: no `.hmd/` directory above it.
  > **Initialize Vault…**

  The preview's empty state carries the same button. Running a cell on such a
  card shows the same choice before `hql` is called, so the user never sees the
  `not a vault` message.

## Initialize Vault

Init answers one question: **where does the vault go?** That is the vault path,
the directory that will hold `.hmd/`.

- A quick pick lists every directory from the workspace folder down to the
  card's own directory. These are the places where a `.hmd/` would claim the
  card. The workspace folder is first and is the default. `doc/` is marked
  when the card is under it, because it is the usual place for a project's
  documentation.
- Each entry shows its path relative to the workspace folder and how many
  `.hmd` files the new vault would claim, so a user can see that a vault at the
  root also claims the cards in `examples/`.
- Confirming writes one empty directory, `<chosen>/.hmd/`, and nothing else.
  The specification's defaults need no `config.toml`.
- Then the extension forgets its cached discovery, re-indexes, and re-renders
  the preview, so the card is in its vault without a reload.
- The same action is the command **HyperMarkDown: Initialize Vault**. From the
  command palette with no card open, it offers the workspace folder and its
  `doc/` directory.

`location_path` is not part of this. It is a setting inside a vault's
`config.toml`, it is read relative to the vault, and before init there is no
vault for it to be relative to. `hmd init` takes it as an option beside the
vault path, for the user who wants the cards in a subfolder from the start.
The extension's init does not need it.

## How the open questions were settled

- **A refused vault.** When the nearest `.hmd/` has a refused `config.toml`, the
  card has a vault and the problem is the config. The refusal is already shown
  once, naming the key. Opening such a card offers nothing more, and running a
  cell on it says the vault is not opened because its config was refused. No
  second marker is offered.
- **A nested vault.** It does not arise. The candidates are the directories
  above a card that no vault claims, so none of them is inside a vault's
  namespace root. A directory that already holds `.hmd/` is left out of the
  list.
- **The cache.** Both paths are fixed. Init rebuilds the catalog itself, and a
  watcher on `**/.hmd` rebuilds it when a marker is created or removed any
  other way. An open preview then finds its card again in the rebuilt catalog.
- **Untrusted workspaces.** Initializing a vault writes to the disk, so it
  follows the extension's rule for writes: it needs a trusted workspace. In
  Restricted Mode the error is shown without the button.
- **Repositories that relied on the fallback.** The changelog states the change
  as breaking, and the error's button is the migration. Every example tree in
  this repository already has its own `.hmd/`, and so does `doc/`.

## Done when

- A card with no `.hmd/` above it, and no `hyperMarkdown.root` set, is not read.
  The preview and the cell runner both show **Vault not found** with
  **Initialize Vault…**.
- The quick pick lists the workspace folder, `doc/` when present, and every
  directory down to the card, and it defaults to the workspace folder.
- Confirming creates an empty `.hmd/` and nothing else. The card is then
  claimed by the new vault, its links resolve, and its cells run, all without a
  reload.
- **HyperMarkDown: Initialize Vault** is in the command palette.
- The README states the rule (no vault, no reading), describes the button and
  the command, and no longer sends extension users to `hmd init`.
- Tests cover the candidate list (card at the root, card under `doc/`, a
  nested card, a card under a refused vault), the error when no vault is found,
  the explicit `hyperMarkdown.root` still working, and the cache being dropped
  after init.

## Not in this card

- A starter `index.hmd`.
- Syntax highlighting, which is a separate question.
